@@ -0,0 +1,26 @@
+---
+fixes:
+  - |
+    PowerOps host recovery now waits for Nova to report exactly one matching
+    source ``nova-compute`` service as ``disabled/down`` after Ironic has
+    confirmed stable power off. The existing ``ironic_fence`` task owns this
+    check, so both automatic and reserved-host flows use it without changing
+    their configured task lists. The pre-fencing service-update delay remains
+    unchanged; it is not evidence that Nova has observed the later power off.
+    The new ``[powerops] nova_down_timeout`` option defaults to 180 seconds;
+    ``poll_interval`` (default 5 seconds) controls polling. The deadline starts
+    after fencing and bounds Nova authentication, API reads and polling sleeps.
+    API reads run in a native worker so a blocked resolver cannot hold the
+    recovery event loop. A read already running when the deadline expires may
+    finish later, but cannot trigger evacuation or power changes; queued reads
+    do not start after the deadline. An inline/disabled thread pool is rejected.
+    Existing coordination and TaskFlow persistence callbacks remain in the
+    recovery context with their existing backend timeout semantics. This option
+    is not a new hard wall-clock limit for those backends or the entire recovery.
+    A service which remains up until the deadline, missing or ambiguous service
+    identity, invalid service state, API failure or lost coordination prevents
+    downstream instance recovery. The check never sets ``forced_down`` and
+    never automatically powers the host back on. Nova and Masakari maintenance
+    settings are not undone on failure; emergency return remains manual.
+    This check observes Nova's view of source liveness; it neither proves
+    destination capacity nor replaces Ironic fencing or stale-domain checks.
